===Etymology===
From {{confix|en|oneiro|t1=''prefix meaning ‘dream’''|mancy|t2=''suffix meaning ‘divination’''}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/əˈnaɪɹə(ʊ)ˌmænsi/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/oʊˈnaɪɹəˌmæn(t)si/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|onei|ro|man|cy|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|oneiromancies}}

# {{lb|en|divination}} [[divination|Divination]] by the [[interpretation]] of [[dream#Noun|dreams]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Samuel Jackson|chapter=Of Sleep and Dreams|title=The Principles of Medicine, Founded on the Structure and Functions of the Animal Organism|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Henry Charles Carey|Carey]] & [[w:Isaac Lea|Lea]]|year=1832|section=§&nbsp;2 (Of Dreams)|page=306|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gtI0AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA306|oclc=2698716|passage=The general tenor of ideas in dreaming is the same as that with which the mind is occupied awake; and thus may be revealed, in dreams, the state of the mind, the secret hopes and wishes, the profoundly hidden thought, and often the circumstances of the dreamer. The knowledge of this fact, and its dextrous application, by an acute observer, with the credulous, compose the art of '''oneiromancy'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=T. Fahd|chapter=KIHĀNA|editors=[[w:Clifford Edmund Bosworth|C[lifford] E[dmund] Bosworth]], E[meri J.] van Donzel, [[w:Bernard Lewis|B[ernard] Lewis]], and [[w:Charles Pellat|Ch[arles] Pellat]]|title=[[w:Encyclopaedia of Islam|The Encyclopaedia of Islam]]|edition=new|location=Leiden|publisher=[[w:Brill Publishers|E. J. Brill]]|year=1979|volume=V|section=fascicules 79–80 (ḴH̱EMS̱H̱IL–ḴIRĀ’A)|page=101|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cJQ3AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA101|column=1|oclc=917237494|passage=The absence of '''oneiromancy''' among the divinatory techniques is justified by the fact that it occupies in Islam a higher place than that of divination in the sphere of religious inspiration.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=A[braham] P. Bos|authorlink=Abraham Bos|chapter=''Manteia'' in Aristotle|title=Cosmic and Meta-cosmic Theology in Aristotle’s Lost Dialogues|series=Brill’s Studies in Intellectual History|seriesvolume=16|year=1989|page=164|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=t3AGz3wyjwgC&pg=PA164|isbn=978-90-04-09155-9|passage=7. ''Did {{w|Aristotle}}'s attitude to '''oneiromancy''' change?'' / In the foregoing sections we sketched the consistent and lucid oneirology which various indirect sources attribute to Aristotle.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Jean-Marie Husser|author2=Jill M. Munro, transl.|chapter=|title=Dreams and Dream Narratives in the Biblical World|series=The Biblical Seminar|seriesvolume=63|location=Sheffield, South Yorkshire|publisher={{w|Sheffield Academic Press}}|year=1999|page=30|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7vpMI0pi_KQC&pg=PA30|isbn=978-1-85075-968-3|passage=2. Deductive '''Oneiromancy''' / This form of '''oneiromancy''' aims to decipher the messages contained in ordinary, everyday dreams, messages thought to presage future events in the life of the dreamer.}}

